COMMAND NAME: gpdeletepart

Drops date partitioned child tables a number of months, 
days, hours from now to maintain a rolling window of data.
The parent table referenced must have been partitioned 
using gpcreatepart or gpaddpart. This utility does not 
support numeric range partitioned or list partitioned tables.

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpdeletepart -t <schema.parent_table> -r <rolling_window_count> 
[-s <fallback_table_name>] [-f] [-q] [-a] [-l <log_dir>] [-D] 
[-d <master_data_directory>] [-x <database>]

gpdeletepart -? | -v

*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpdeletepart script drops child tables from a parent table 
that was partitioned using gpcreatepart or gpaddpart. This is 
useful if you have a date partitioned table and want to maintain a 
rolling window of data, for example the last 12 months from today. 
The script will drop the oldest child table partitions (and associated 
indexes and rewrite rules) that are outside of the specified rolling 
window. The rolling window start date is calculated from the current 
database date. This script can only be used on date partitioned tables 
(parent tables partitioned on a timestamp or date column). It does 
not support numeric range, list partitioned tables, or rolling window 
ranges not based on today’s date and time.

To delete child table partitions that fall outside of the criteria of 
maintaining a rolling window number of months/days/hours from now, 
use DROP TABLE and DROP RULE to drop the old child tables and 
their associated rewrite rules directly.

*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-t <schema.parent_table>

Required. The name of the schema-qualified parent table from 
which you want to drop child tables.


-r <rolling_window_count>

Required. The number of most recent months, days, hours from 
now that you want to keep. Older child table partitions outside 
this window will be dropped. The rolling window start date and 
time is calculated from the current database date and time. For 
example, on a monthly partition scheme, if the current date is 
2008-02-01 and you want to keep the past 12 months of data, all 
child tables and records older than 2007-03-01 will be dropped.


-s <fallback_table_name>

Specifies the name of a table where child table records will 
be copied before the child table is dropped. This provides some 
fall back in case you unintentionally delete partitioned data 
you wanted to keep.


-f (force cascaded delete)

If you have a nested partition design, this will force the 
deletion of child tables that are also parents to other tables 
(DROP CASCADE). The default is to not allow the removal of 
child tables that are also parents to other tables.


-q (no output)

Do not output messages to the screen. Write messages to log file only.


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-l log_directory

Specifies an alternative directory to write the log file. By default 
log files are written to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets logging level to debug.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show script version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of 
this script.

*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************


Drop all monthly partitioned tables that are older than 18 months 
from today (and force the deletion of any nested child tables on 
the months that are dropped).

gpdeletepart -t dw.sales -r 12 -f

